FACE-BASED SNAP GEOMETRY

Extend Timber Beam connection geometry beyond endpoint points.

Each Timber Beam should expose local-space geometry including:

- END_A_CENTER
- END_B_CENTER
- END_A_FACE
- END_B_FACE
- TOP_FACE
- BOTTOM_FACE
- SIDE_A_FACE
- SIDE_B_FACE
- Optional joint-capable side sockets
- Local longitudinal axis
- Local face normals

Blocks should expose compatible:

- Top face
- Bottom face
- Left face
- Right face
- Corners or edge endpoints where useful
- Joint-capable surfaces where permitted

Transform all geometry through authoritative world position and rotation.

Do not define angled behavior through screen coordinates or special-case 45
degrees only.

The system should work across supported angle increments.

FLUSH ANGLED-BEAM-TO-FLAT-FACE CONNECTION

When an angled beam endpoint approaches a compatible flat face:

1. Identify the beam endpoint face.
2. Identify the target face and normal.
3. Calculate the transform that places the beam endpoint flush against the target
   face.
4. Preserve the player-selected beam angle.
5. Avoid penetration.
6. Avoid visible gaps beyond documented tolerance.
7. Show a FACE JOINT preview.
8. Commit the appropriate breakable joint on valid release.

Examples:

- 45-degree rafter meeting the top of a wall block
- 45-degree brace meeting the side of a vertical post
- Angled beam meeting a horizontal roof beam
- Angled beam meeting a stone or wood support face when the material rules permit
  attachment

For this milestone, Timber Beam endpoints may attach structurally to:

- Timber Beam joint-capable faces
- Wood Block faces
- Existing timber-compatible framing sockets

A Timber Beam resting on stone may remain supported contact unless the current
game explicitly supports timber-to-stone attachment.

Do not create invisible adhesive on arbitrary stone surfaces without a defined
connection rule.

FACE ALIGNMENT TOLERANCE

Use socket/face-specific tolerances.

Permit:

- Boundary contact
- Small visual seam
- Small documented numerical clearance

Reject:

- Collider interior penetration
- Beam body crossing the target body
- Attachment where the remaining beam intersects another piece
- Attachment outside the build zone
- Attachment through terrain

Do not broadly shrink colliders.

ROOF PEAK RECOGNITION

Recognize a roof-peak candidate when:

- Two Timber Beam endpoints approach one another.
- The beam slopes are opposed.
- Their endpoint faces can meet without material overlap.
- Their longitudinal axes create a plausible peak angle.
- Neither beam penetrates the other.
- The player intentionally releases within the peak snap range.

Do not require exactly +45 and -45 degrees.

Support a documented range of opposing roof slopes, such as:

- Approximately 25 to 65 degrees on each side
- Compatible included peak angle
- Stable endpoint-face alignment

Use world-space geometry rather than hardcoded angle labels.

ROOF PEAK PRESENTATION

During preview:

- Highlight both endpoint faces.
- Show “Roof Peak Joint.”
- Preview a small connector, binding, cap, or joinery symbol.
- Show whether lower supports are currently detected.
- Do not promise stability.

After placement:

- Preserve both manual angles.
- Create one logical Roof Peak Joint.
- Do not add duplicate ordinary endpoint joints at the same socket pair.

ROOF PEAK PHYSICS

The Roof Peak Joint should:

- Keep the apex together under ordinary supported roof load.
- Permit small realistic compliance.
- Transfer force between the rafters.
- Benefit structurally when lower beam ends are supported.
- Become vulnerable when lower supports spread.
- Be vulnerable to direct projectile impact.
- Be vulnerable to asymmetric roof collapse.
- Break independently from either beam where appropriate.

Do not make Roof Peak inherently stronger in every force direction.

Recommended relative behavior:

- Better than an ordinary collinear splice under roof compression.
- Not necessarily better under tension or sideways shear.
- Worse when the lower supports are absent or moving outward.

If the existing physics engine cannot model directional strength directly,
approximate it with centralized, documented joint limits and geometry-aware
stress evaluation.

Do not use a hidden invulnerability multiplier.

DIAGONAL BRACE CONNECTION

Recognize a brace candidate when an angled Timber Beam meaningfully connects:

- A vertical post to a horizontal beam
- A post to a block-supported frame
- Two compatible frame members

A valid brace should create intentional joints at one or both endpoints.

If only one endpoint is connected:

- The remaining endpoint behaves according to contact or free physics.

If both endpoints are connected:

- The brace may resist lateral deformation.
- It must remain breakable.
- It must not lock the whole construction into a fully rigid body.

Show “Brace Joint” during preview where appropriate.

Do not automatically classify every angled beam as a brace.

T-JOINT SUPPORT

Provide intentional endpoint-to-side joining for Timber Beams.

A T-joint requires:

- Moving beam endpoint
- Compatible target side socket or joint-capable face
- Valid geometric alignment
- No material overlap
- Deliberate release while the preview is active

A T-joint must not be created merely because a beam endpoint touches any point
along another beam.

Use discrete, visible joint-capable side locations or a well-defined continuous
face rule.

Prefer a small number of predictable side sockets for the initial implementation.

JOINT GRAPH OWNERSHIP

Maintain one authoritative logical joint graph.

Requirements:

- Stable joint IDs
- One joint per accepted socket pair
- No duplicate constraints
- No UI component owns authoritative joints
- Physics constraints derive from the logical graph
- Construction preview uses a transaction overlay
- Persistence stores logical joints, not physics-engine constraint objects
- Snapshot restore rebuilds constraints exactly once
- Replay does not create authoritative joints

JOINT COMPATIBILITY AFTER EDIT

When the selected piece is released:

For each original joint:

- If the socket relationship remains geometrically compatible, preserve the
  joint ID and profile where appropriate.
- If it becomes incompatible, remove it.
- If the connection type changes, replace it atomically with a new joint type.
- Do not stretch a joint beyond valid tolerances.
- Do not leave stale references.

For each new previewed joint:

- Validate final geometry.
- Reject duplicate socket ownership when a socket allows only one joint.
- Create one logical joint.

If release is invalid:

- Restore all original joints.

MULTI-JOINT PIECES

A Timber Beam may have more than one connection.

Examples:

- A rafter with a wall-face joint at its lower end and Roof Peak Joint at its
  upper end
- A diagonal brace connected at both ends
- A horizontal beam supported by two posts
- A center beam with two connected extensions

Editing such a beam:

- Temporarily previews all affected relationships.
- Keeps unaffected neighboring structures stationary.
- Restores all original joints on cancel.
- Atomically commits the final valid joint set on release.

Do not require detaching each joint individually before movement.

CONNECTION STRENGTH AND SUPPORT CONTEXT

Do not decide stability solely from joint type.

The effective structural outcome depends on:

- Joint strength profile
- Piece mass
- Span length
- Number and location of supports
- Force direction
- Torque
- Load position
- Impact severity
- Neighboring joint state

A peaked roof supported at both lower ends should perform better than two
unsupported rafters joined in midair.

An unsupported connected roof may:

- Fall as a connected assembly
- Pivot
- Spread
- Strain
- Break at the peak
- Break at a lower face joint

Do not artificially suspend unsupported construction.

