SNAP HYSTERESIS

Use candidate locking/hysteresis to prevent flicker.

Once a candidate is acquired:

- Retain it within a larger release radius.
- Do not jump rapidly between splice, face, and peak candidates.
- Release only after clear movement away or invalid geometry.
- Preserve selected rotation throughout.

Do not make the snap feel sticky across large distances.

COLLISION VALIDATION

Every proposed edit remains subject to:

- Full collider overlap validation
- Swept movement/rotation validation where applicable
- Terrain boundaries
- Build-zone boundaries
- Fixed geometry
- Other construction pieces
- Active wisps when editing during reinforcement
- Existing connection constraints

No joint type may authorize material overlap.

At a valid shared connection plane:

- Boundary contact is permitted.
- Interior penetration is rejected.

STABILITY TEST INTEGRATION

When Test Stability begins:

- Instantiate each logical joint exactly once.
- Use the correct constraint behavior per joint type.
- Initialize without penetration.
- Reset stale forces and velocities as already accepted.
- Allow joint compliance, strain, and failure.
- Include meaningful joint motion in settlement detection.
- Ignore tiny harmless flex for eventual sleep/settlement.

After Restore Design:

- Remove live constraints.
- Restore logical joint definitions.
- Rebuild subsequent test constraints exactly once.

Repeated testing must not increase energy, joint counts, or body counts.

JOINT FAILURE

Preserve the existing joint states and extend them where needed:

- INTACT
- STRAINED
- BROKEN

Failure must:

- Remove the relevant physics constraint once.
- Preserve piece bodies.
- Not automatically destroy either beam.
- Record attack or load cause.
- Remain broken during that siege/reinforcement attempt.
- Not become repairable in this milestone.

Different joint types may fail under different force directions.

Keep formulas centralized and documented.

REINFORCEMENT

Use the same intuitive editing system during reinforcement.

Requirements:

- Intact connected pieces may be selected and moved through joint-edit preview.
- Unchanged compatible joints may be preserved.
- Incompatible joints are removed on valid release.
- New joints may be created.
- Broken joints remain broken.
- Broken debris cannot create joints.
- Manual movement and rotation cannot overlap surviving wisps.
- Reinforcement history remains separate.
- Cancel restores the reinforcement-entry relationship for that edit.

Do not implement repair.

UNDO AND REDO

Treat one ordinary connected-piece edit as one atomic history operation containing:

- Previous transform
- New transform
- Removed joints
- Preserved joints
- Created joints
- Previous construction revision
- New construction revision

Undo must restore the exact previous transform and joint graph.

Redo must:

- Restore the accepted committed result
- Avoid duplicate joints
- Revalidate only where necessary for safe restoration
- Never partially restore the operation

Cancel and rejected edits create no history entry.

Explicit Detach remains its own undoable operation.

